INT. HOPE VALLEY FERTILITY CLINIC - WAITING ROOM - MORNING

We see a bright, quiet waiting room. Pale green walls. A fish tank bubbling in the corner. A rack of old magazines no one has touched. Sunlight comes through the blinds in thin stripes.

CLOSE ON a wall clock. It reads 9:02.

BETH, 30s, nervous, in a yellow cardigan, sits on the edge of a chair, twisting a tissue. Next to her is CAL, 30s, tall, dressed too neatly, holding a folder of forms.

CAL
(smiling too hard, patting her knee)
It's going to be great.

BETH
(whispering)
You said that about the camping trip.

CAL
(a beat)
Okay, that was a different kind of great.

The fish tank bubbles. A little orange fish swims in a circle. Beth watches it as if it knows something.

INTERCOM (V.O.)
Beth and Cal Whitaker, Dr. Okafor will see you now. Room three, please.

BETH
(standing, dropping the tissue)
Oh gosh. That's us. That's definitely us.

CAL
(picking it up, handing it back)
It's us. Breathe.

INT. DR. OKAFOR'S OFFICE - CONTINUOUS

A warm room. Diplomas on the wall. A box of tissues, strategically placed. A model of a uterus on the desk that Cal keeps looking at, then looking away from.

DR. OKAFOR, 50s, kind eyes, silver glasses, stands to greet them.

DR. OKAFOR
(shaking Beth's hand, then Cal's)
Please, sit. I'm Dr. Okafor. I've read your file. How are you feeling?

BETH
(sitting, folding her hands)
Fine! Good. Super.

CAL
(sitting, crossing his legs, then uncrossing)
Fantastic. Never better.

DR. OKAFOR
(gently, with a small smile)
You're both very brave to be here.

BETH
(laughing, nervous)
We are not. We are very afraid.

CAL
(nodding hard)
We're terrified.

Both speak at once.

BETH
It's been three years.

^CAL
It's been three years.

They look at each other. The room goes quiet for a second.

DR. OKAFOR
(taking off her glasses)
That is a long time to hope.

Beth's face crumples a little. Cal takes her hand, and she lets him.

BETH
(wiping her eye)
Sorry. I told myself I wouldn't do this.

DR. OKAFOR
(sliding the tissue box across)
Everyone does. It's one of the reasons I keep a big box.

The door opens a crack. TINA, 20s, a nurse in cartoon scrubs, leans in.

TINA
(whispering, apologetic)
Doctor, I'm so sorry. The lab is on the phone.

DR. OKAFOR
(to the couple)
Excuse me one moment.

She picks up the desk phone. We hear a faint voice through it.

TINA (V.O.)
(through the phone, tinny)
It's about the samples from last week, Doctor. They've come back.

DR. OKAFOR
(listening, her face changing)
(beat)
Thank you, Tina. I'll tell them myself.

She puts the phone down very slowly. Beth grips Cal's hand so hard his fingers go white.

BETH
(very small)
Is it bad?

DR. OKAFOR
(smiling, for the first time all morning)
Beth. Cal. I think you should both take a deep breath.

Outside, the fish swims once around the tank and stops. The light in the blinds turns gold.

CAL
(breathless)
Is that a yes?

DR. OKAFOR
(nodding)
It's a yes.

Beth bursts into tears. Cal laughs, jumps up, knocks over the model on the desk, and catches it in the air.

CAL (CONT'D)
(holding it up like a trophy)
Sorry! I got it! I got it!

BETH
(laughing through tears)
Cal, sit DOWN.

He sits. They hold each other. Dr. Okafor looks away to give them privacy and quietly dabs her own eyes.

Sunlight. Quiet. Somewhere, a baby cries in another room and everyone smiles.
